   "nrr:confidence": {
    "level": "low",
    "basis": "one mouse per trial, five mice in total, no statistics, no detection limit and no blinding reported; the pub is framed as a demonstration rather than a powered study"
   },
   "nrr:outcomeSummary": "Touch produced localized contralateral somatosensory signals and capsaicin produced widespread oscillatory cortical activation absent in vehicle trials; histamine produced activation indistinguishable from saline and inconsistent between trials, so the itch application was iced.",
   "nrr:hypothesis": "Determine whether in vivo flavoprotein autofluorescence imaging can measure brain responses to touch, capsaicin-induced pain and histamine-induced itch, as a behaviour-independent alternative to mouse behavioural assays.",
   "nrr:nextSteps": [
    "Repeat the itch experiments with a sensitive genetically encoded calcium indicator such as GCaMP to increase signal, testing the hypothesis that too few itch-responsive neurons produce too little autofluorescence to detect above noise.",
    "Perform the experiments on non-anesthetized animals, because anesthesia is known to suppress cortical activity (ref 33).",
    "Repeat the capsaicin experiments to distinguish activity patterns inherent to the brain from those caused by experimental variability, and determine what injection location, dose and prior-injection factors contribute to the oscillations.",
    "Test analgesic drugs to see whether they alleviate pain-related sensory activity in the brain, as a check on drug-screening applicability.",
    "Perform this imaging on chronic mouse pain and itch models rather than the acute models used here."
   ],
